<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Heath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Heath was a British Conservative politician and prime minister of the United Kingdom from 1970 to 1974. His government took the country into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1973 but struggled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and industrial conflict, and a miners' strike and the three-day week led to his defeat in 1974; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> replaced him as party leader in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-heath.jpg" alt="Edward Heath" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan Warren · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Heath_4_Allan_Warren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Richard George Heath was born at Broadstairs, Kent, on 9 July 1916, the son of William George Heath, a carpenter who later became a builder and a small businessman, and Edith Anne Heath, a lady's maid. He was educated at Chatham House Grammar School in Ramsgate and in 1935 went up to Balliol College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, with a county scholarship. A talented musician, he won the college's organ scholarship and graduated in philosophy, politics and economics in 1939. In 1941 he was commissioned in the Royal Artillery, serving with anti-aircraft guns, and he became a civil servant after the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Heath was elected Member of Parliament for Bexley in 1950. As Chief Whip from 1955 he kept out of the Suez controversy, and his report on Conservative opinion helped secure the premiership for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a> in 1957. He served as Minister of Labour and as President of the Board of Trade. In July 1965 he unexpectedly won the leadership of the Conservative Party, with 150 votes to Reginald Maudling's 133 and Enoch Powell's 15, and he remained leader after the defeat of 1966. In 1970 his party won 330 seats to Labour's 287.</p><p>As prime minister he completed the decimalisation of British coinage and reformed local government in 1972. Unemployment passed one million in January 1972, and Heath reversed course in what became known as the Barber Boom. The Industrial Relations Act 1971 set up a special court whose imprisonment of striking dockworkers was a public relations disaster. His government took the United Kingdom into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> on 1 January 1973. During the Troubles he held unofficial talks with the Provisional IRA in July 1972, which failed, and later pressed for a settlement with parties committed to nonviolence.</p><p>Heath called an election for 28 February 1974 under the slogan Who governs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>? The result gave the Conservatives the most votes but Labour slightly more seats. Talks with Jeremy Thorpe's Liberal Party failed and Heath resigned on 4 March. After a second defeat in October 1974 he lost the leadership to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> in February 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Heath campaigned with Thatcher for the yes side in the 1975 referendum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> and served on the Brandt Commission. He later opposed Thatcherism. A keen yachtsman, he won the 1969 Sydney to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a> race and captained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s winning Admiral's Cup team in 1971 while prime minister. He was also an organist, pianist and conductor. He never married, published The Course of My Life in 1998 and left the Commons in 2001. He died on 17 July 2005, aged 89.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Heath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Having declined to join the European Economic Community at its foundation, the UK entered it in 1973 under <b>Edward Heath</b> and confirmed membership by referendum in 1975.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Broadstairs, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Salisbury, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.36,1.44&amp;year=1973" aria-label="Broadstairs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4583 385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Heath"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5033 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5033 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.36,1.44&amp;year=1973">What was here in 1973? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
